        "final_response": "I didn't make any changes this round, because I can't find a regression in my refactor. I haven't verified the cause.\n\nI compared the current `execute` and its four helpers (`validate`, `fetchPrice`, `applyToUser`, `publish`) against the original line by line. Validation, the order of calls, the exception-to-status mapping, the saved trade, the RabbitMQ publish and the 201 response are all the same. `Clients.java`, `UserController.apply` and `UserService.apply` are untouched and consistent with that flow.\n\nThe 400 on `/api/trades/buy` could come from several places, and the response body doesn't say which:\n- `validate` rejecting the request.\n- `fetchPrice` catching any `FeignException`, including a transport or load-balancer failure, and returning 400 \"invalid symbol\".\n- `applyToUser` mapping a 400 from user-service to \"trade rejected\".\n- A request-body parse error, which also gives a generic 400.\n\nSpring hides the error message in the response body, so I can't tell these apart from the evidence I have.\n\nThe market-service log excerpt shows Eureka registration failures at 05:12:05. Those cleared by 05:12:35, but they point to a start-up timing problem. If trade-service couldn't reach market-service or user-service through Eureka or the Feign load balancer, the original code would have hit the same 400 from `fetchPrice`. The truncated trade-service and user-service logs would show whether that is the cause.\n\nI haven't edited anything speculatively. A guess would risk changing behaviour the acceptance suite checks. Could you send the trade-service log around 05:13:17, including any Feign or `ResponseStatusException` trace? It would also help to know whether the baseline passed T05 and T08–T11 before the refactor. If it did, I'd revert to the original `execute` as the safest fallback, which would leave the CCN finding open.",
